WEBVTT

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:47.480
It's September, early spring in the southern hemisphere.

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:53.100
The Antarctic continent is encircled by sea ice that extends for hundreds of miles northwards

00:00:53.100 --> 00:01:00.320
around its coasts and encloses all but a few islands.

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:05.880
But these ice-free islands like South Georgia are very precious, for here the sea never

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:31.960
freezes and any sea animal that needs to can always get a show.

00:01:31.960 --> 00:02:00.040
First to return each spring are the bull elephant seals.

00:02:00.040 --> 00:02:05.400
They are about to land on a breeding beach and each one knows that when he does he will

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:11.000
have to face rivals.

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:16.760
A full-grown male weighs over three tons.

00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:24.820
Half the world's population will come to this one island, 8,000 to this beach alone.

00:02:24.820 --> 00:02:31.080
This immense gathering of elephant seals extends for some two miles up this beach.

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:35.480
At first sight it might seem to be totally disorganized but in fact there's a pattern

00:02:35.480 --> 00:02:36.480
to it.

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:40.040
All these are females.

00:02:40.040 --> 00:02:47.320
They came ashore about a month ago to pup and now they're ready to breed again and they

00:02:47.320 --> 00:02:56.760
all belong to this one male.

00:02:56.760 --> 00:03:04.880
This is a beach master and there are a dozen or so like him spaced out along this beach.

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:11.080
Each one of them has his own harem and I estimate that this one has about a hundred females

00:03:11.080 --> 00:03:17.760
in his and his sole object in life at the moment is to make quite sure that he and he

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:33.920
alone mates with every single one of them and to that he must fight.

00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:53.040
He's won but he'll have to battle many times every day if he is to keep control.

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:56.280
The females gave birth soon after they arrived.

00:03:56.280 --> 00:04:00.840
They now have three weeks in which to feed their pups before they themselves have to

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:06.200
go back to sea to feed.

00:04:06.200 --> 00:04:13.240
In that short time they have to transform a near empty bag of skin into a full bag of

00:04:13.240 --> 00:04:17.680
blubber.

00:04:17.680 --> 00:04:22.120
As they come to the end of suckling the females become sexually receptive again.

00:04:22.120 --> 00:04:32.400
That is the moment that the beach master has been waiting for.

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:40.680
But while he is busy arrival is also busy on the edge of the harem and that can't be

00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:41.680
tolerated.

00:04:41.680 --> 00:05:03.960
A roar is enough.

00:05:03.960 --> 00:05:10.560
The interloper retreats.

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:13.880
But many conflicts will only be settled by violence.

00:05:13.880 --> 00:05:27.820
Males get ripped and those that get in the way of the fighters are likely to get crushed.

00:05:27.820 --> 00:05:32.340
Every now and then the beach master proclaims his dominance with a roar.

00:05:32.340 --> 00:05:35.440
The bigger the ball the louder and deeper his voice.

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:49.600
A rival can judge from it whether or not he has a chance in a straight battle.

00:05:49.600 --> 00:05:57.720
If he is going to persevere with his challenge he must now fight.

00:05:57.720 --> 00:06:00.840
The pair rear up to over eight feet.

00:06:00.840 --> 00:06:07.840
Their only weapons are their teeth but they can do a lot of damage.

00:06:30.840 --> 00:06:37.880
The hide on the neck is particularly thick and prevents serious injury.

00:06:37.880 --> 00:06:42.080
A bout can go on for a quarter of an hour.

00:06:42.080 --> 00:06:56.760
Eventually the battle is brought to an end by exhaustion as much as anything else.

00:06:56.760 --> 00:07:02.960
From the grassy slopes above the battleground the scene is more peaceful.

00:07:02.960 --> 00:07:31.320
Black-browed albatross are returning from the sea.

00:07:31.320 --> 00:07:36.840
Gray-headed albatross are here too hanging on the updrafts caused when the ever-continuing

00:07:36.840 --> 00:07:53.320
wind is deflected upwards by the cliff face.

00:07:53.320 --> 00:07:58.240
Throughout the past winter these birds have been flying alone over the vast ocean searching

00:07:58.240 --> 00:07:59.640
for food.

00:07:59.640 --> 00:08:11.160
But now they're returning to breed and are assembling in colonies several thousand strong.

00:08:11.160 --> 00:08:17.740
Breeding pairs from previous seasons are reunited and each uses exactly the same nest mound

00:08:17.740 --> 00:08:19.740
as they used before.

00:08:19.740 --> 00:08:28.640
But it does need a bit of renovation.

00:08:28.640 --> 00:08:39.520
Mutual grooming renews the bond between them.

00:08:39.520 --> 00:08:45.520
Both gray-headed and black-browed albatross are faithful for life, twenty years or so,

00:08:45.520 --> 00:08:49.920
and long-established pairs only need a brief repetition of their courtship ritual before

00:08:49.920 --> 00:09:06.640
they mate.

00:09:06.640 --> 00:09:12.920
Two weeks later the female lays a single egg and for the next seventy days the two take

00:09:12.920 --> 00:09:26.160
turns to incubate it.

00:09:26.160 --> 00:09:31.840
While one keeps the egg warm the other flies off to feed and may have to travel thousands

00:09:31.840 --> 00:09:38.680
of miles before it gets what it needs.

00:09:38.680 --> 00:09:44.680
Most kinds of albatross nest in colonies, one special one however prefers a more solitary

00:09:44.680 --> 00:09:50.280
life.

00:09:50.280 --> 00:09:55.040
Light-mantled sooty albatross are the last to return to the island.

00:09:55.040 --> 00:10:03.380
The males come first.

00:10:03.380 --> 00:10:27.920
One that is still unpaired settles on a ledge and calls to passing females.

00:10:27.920 --> 00:10:37.920
Having listened to many she eventually selects one.

00:10:37.920 --> 00:10:49.640
The next stage in courtship involves a certain amount of nodding and dancing.

00:10:49.640 --> 00:11:13.520
And then there follows a most beautiful, perfectly synchronised display flight.

00:11:13.520 --> 00:11:38.480
Often

00:11:38.480 --> 00:11:55.320
During the day, the skies belong to the albatross, but as darkness comes, other, more nervous

00:11:55.320 --> 00:12:06.920
and more numerous birds come to the island.

00:12:06.920 --> 00:12:15.480
Millions of small petrels and prions fly agitatedly around the cliffs in the darkness.

00:12:15.480 --> 00:12:24.160
Twenty-two million nest among the tussock grass on the island of South Georgia alone.

00:12:24.160 --> 00:12:29.400
Being so small, the prions are vulnerable to attack by skewers, and for the safety of

00:12:29.400 --> 00:12:34.640
the defenseless chicks during the day, they make their nest in burrows.

00:12:34.640 --> 00:12:45.600
Outside, the white-chinned petrels assemble.

00:12:45.600 --> 00:12:50.920
Duetting pairs defend the territories around their burrows that can extend two meters beneath

00:12:50.920 --> 00:12:58.880
the tussock grass.

00:12:58.880 --> 00:13:03.360
The chick stays safely inside the burrow for two months.

00:13:03.360 --> 00:13:12.360
The other day, one of the adults comes to feed it with a mixture of squid and krill.

00:13:12.360 --> 00:13:18.040
Before dawn and danger, all the adults will have disappeared from the island and returned

00:13:18.040 --> 00:13:26.760
to the open ocean.

00:13:26.760 --> 00:13:32.520
This hillside is jam packed with macaroni penguins and virtually nothing else.

00:13:32.520 --> 00:13:36.360
There are some 80,000 of them here.

00:13:36.360 --> 00:13:41.720
But even this vast assemblage is only a tiny proportion of the total population of South

00:13:41.720 --> 00:13:46.000
Georgia, which is estimated to be more than 10 million.

00:13:46.000 --> 00:13:51.720
It's an astonishing demonstration of the fact that although the Antarctic is virtually

00:13:51.720 --> 00:13:59.920
lifeless over vast areas, there are one or two smaller oases that team with life.

00:13:59.920 --> 00:14:11.960
After spending the winter wandering around the northern fringes of the Southern Ocean,

00:14:11.960 --> 00:14:15.680
the macaroni return with remarkable punctuality.

00:14:15.680 --> 00:14:21.800
In just 10 days, the terraces of this empty stadium become packed tight.

00:14:21.800 --> 00:14:29.560
The males come first, the females a week later.

00:14:29.560 --> 00:14:35.880
The macaroni is very much the penguin of the northern rim of the Antarctic.

00:14:35.880 --> 00:14:41.000
Very few of them venture farther south than the sub-Antarctic islands.

00:14:41.000 --> 00:14:46.640
But here, they constitute over 50% of all the sea birds.

00:14:46.640 --> 00:14:51.760
At the moment, at the beginning of the breeding season, they're squabbling noisily as each

00:14:51.760 --> 00:14:58.800
pair fights to hold its own tiny nest strike.

00:14:58.800 --> 00:15:08.120
Each new arrival has to make its way through a barrage of pecks from outraged nest owners.

00:15:08.120 --> 00:15:12.880
Macaronis must be the noisiest and most bad tempered of all penguins, and sometimes the

00:15:12.880 --> 00:15:32.120
fights can be really vicious.

00:15:32.120 --> 00:15:59.840
Eventually, a female finds her male and is rewarded with a greeting display and a comforting

00:15:59.840 --> 00:16:10.600
preen.

00:16:10.600 --> 00:16:14.400
Ten days later, she's produced two eggs.

00:16:14.400 --> 00:16:20.080
But remarkably, one of them, the darker, smaller one, is nearly always abandoned.

00:16:20.080 --> 00:16:21.960
Why is not certain.

00:16:21.960 --> 00:16:26.920
It may perhaps be an insurance against the loss of the bigger one.

00:16:26.920 --> 00:16:29.920
But the colony is not littered with abandoned eggs.

00:16:29.920 --> 00:16:42.040
It has its own squad of refuse collectors, sheathbills.

00:16:42.040 --> 00:16:47.480
During the summer, they normally eat penguin droppings, an abandoned, if addled, egg must

00:16:47.480 --> 00:16:52.200
make a nice change.

00:16:52.200 --> 00:16:56.200
Sheathbills are one of the few birds here that do not rely on the ocean for food, at

00:16:56.200 --> 00:16:57.200
least directly.

00:16:57.200 --> 00:17:00.200
They are totally land based.

00:17:00.200 --> 00:17:07.960
All the wildlife here in South Georgia, the macaroni penguins, the albatrosses, the elephant

00:17:07.960 --> 00:17:14.320
seals, even the tussock grass, is virtually restricted to the outer rim of Antarctica.

00:17:14.320 --> 00:17:17.040
Farther south, it's a harsher world.

00:17:17.040 --> 00:17:19.480
There, ice dominates.

00:17:19.480 --> 00:17:23.480
But with the arrival of spring, that world is warming just slightly.

00:17:23.480 --> 00:17:29.000
The sea ice is retreating and animals are returning, animals that are specially adapted

00:17:29.000 --> 00:17:36.440
to life in the frozen south.

00:17:36.440 --> 00:17:40.080
Most of Antarctica is still locked in by sea ice.

00:17:40.080 --> 00:17:44.520
But as the days lengthen, so that slowly retreats.

00:17:44.520 --> 00:17:49.840
First to be freed is the Antarctic Peninsula, a long arm of the continent that reaches up

00:17:49.840 --> 00:17:57.120
northwards.

00:17:57.120 --> 00:18:01.880
For a few months, it's possible to reach its coast by sea.

00:18:01.880 --> 00:18:06.320
Antarctica is nowhere lovelier.

00:18:06.320 --> 00:18:11.480
But even at the height of the summer, only 2% of the continent is free from ice.

00:18:11.480 --> 00:18:28.880
And most of that is here.

00:18:28.880 --> 00:18:50.240
But no sea animals will reach those distant rock slopes until the sea ice breaks up.

00:18:50.240 --> 00:19:00.120
Even two penguins are among the first to make it.

00:19:00.120 --> 00:19:06.120
They need bare rock for their nests, but even now it's so scarce they may have a hard climb

00:19:06.120 --> 00:19:12.880
to reach it.

00:19:12.880 --> 00:19:17.360
These are on their way to relieve their mates, who for the past three days have been looking

00:19:17.360 --> 00:19:24.960
after the eggs.

00:19:24.960 --> 00:19:30.240
Their eggs were laid in November, almost a month after the gentoo's up in South Georgia.

00:19:30.240 --> 00:19:34.920
There's no soil here with which to make a nest, and precious little vegetation, just

00:19:34.920 --> 00:19:43.680
a few small stones.

00:19:43.680 --> 00:20:12.160
And even the stones are in short supply and may have to be borrowed from a neighbour.

00:20:12.160 --> 00:20:19.120
Nobody likes to see their nest disappearing from beneath their feet.

00:20:19.120 --> 00:20:31.120
But when thieves come from all sides, there's not much you can do about it.

00:20:31.120 --> 00:20:41.240
After five weeks of incubation, the chicks start to hatch.

00:20:41.240 --> 00:20:45.320
Unlike the macaroni's, both the gentoo's eggs hatch.

00:20:45.320 --> 00:20:49.920
For three weeks the adults care for the chicks, protecting them from the cold.

00:20:49.920 --> 00:20:57.280
They take turns to bring meals of small fish and krill.

00:20:57.280 --> 00:21:02.640
But the labour of doing so is enormous, for there's that snow slope to be traversed every

00:21:02.640 --> 00:21:26.000
time, and penguins were not designed for skiing.

00:21:26.000 --> 00:21:49.160
As spring advances, more and more of the peninsula becomes ice-free, and humpback whales appear

00:21:49.160 --> 00:22:02.800
along the coast seeking krill.

00:22:02.800 --> 00:22:08.240
The sea ice, as it disintegrates, forms a sort of soup of loose blocks.

00:22:08.240 --> 00:22:25.800
This is the pack ice, the whales will go no further.

00:22:25.800 --> 00:22:30.760
At its outer edges, the pack is easy to get through, but as you go further south, the

00:22:30.760 --> 00:22:48.120
flows become bigger and more closely packed.

00:22:48.120 --> 00:22:52.160
Only the most powerful ice-breaking ships can force a passage through the vast band

00:22:52.160 --> 00:22:54.740
of broken ice that rings the continent.

00:22:54.740 --> 00:22:57.760
In places it's 200 miles across.

00:22:57.760 --> 00:23:03.000
This however, surprisingly, is home of the most numerous large mammal in the world, apart

00:23:03.000 --> 00:23:06.760
from man.

00:23:06.760 --> 00:23:08.680
Crab eater seals.

00:23:08.680 --> 00:23:13.880
Up to 30 million live around the continent in this in-between world of ice and water.

00:23:13.880 --> 00:23:16.200
Here they rest, and here they pup.

00:23:16.200 --> 00:23:19.840
They never come to land.

00:23:19.840 --> 00:23:22.740
Despite their name, they live on krill.

00:23:22.740 --> 00:23:50.520
They sieve seawater through their interlocking teeth and consume 20 kilos of it every day.

00:23:50.520 --> 00:23:56.120
Even farther south, beyond the pack ice, there still remains, mile after mile, of winter

00:23:56.120 --> 00:24:00.120
ice that has not yet broken up.

00:24:00.120 --> 00:24:11.440
Very few creatures can get across this to the land beyond, but one does.

00:24:11.440 --> 00:24:13.720
The Adelie penguin.

00:24:13.720 --> 00:24:17.620
They breed farther south than any other penguin.

00:24:17.620 --> 00:24:21.200
They can't wait for the ice to break and have to walk.

00:24:21.200 --> 00:24:25.420
In some years they will march for over 60 miles to reach their traditional breeding

00:24:25.420 --> 00:24:28.840
grounds.

00:24:28.840 --> 00:24:31.280
The Antarctic summer is short indeed.

00:24:31.280 --> 00:24:32.280
They must hurry.

00:24:32.280 --> 00:24:54.100
Jam-jam early.

00:24:54.100 --> 00:25:13.100
Their journey is remarkable enough, but incredibly, one creature makes an even longer one.

00:25:13.100 --> 00:25:19.020
Snow petrels are smaller than pigeons, yet they fly across ice that never melts and

00:25:19.020 --> 00:25:30.220
climb to altitudes of 3,000 metres, right up and onto the vast Antarctic ice cap.

00:25:30.220 --> 00:25:36.020
Here, over an area larger than Australia, the ice is several miles thick. It blankets

00:25:36.020 --> 00:25:47.620
whole mountain ranges. Only the summits of the tallest project through it, as non-attacks.

00:25:47.620 --> 00:25:53.340
These few tiny patches of rock, isolated in a sea of ice, are as precious as an oasis

00:25:53.340 --> 00:26:00.580
in a desert. Only 2% of the continent is ice-free, and nearly all of that is near the coast.

00:26:00.580 --> 00:26:05.660
But snow petrels, like nearly all Antarctic birds, can't lay their eggs on ice, and they're

00:26:05.660 --> 00:26:11.740
prepared to fly a very long way to find bare rock. One of their nests was found on an unattack

00:26:11.740 --> 00:26:18.740
like this, 144 miles from the coast.

00:26:18.740 --> 00:26:32.740
Snow petrels bring life to this, the most lifeless part of our planet.

00:26:32.740 --> 00:26:37.760
They breed farther south than any other bird. They have to wait for their nesting ledges

00:26:37.760 --> 00:26:42.680
to be cleared from the thick snow. Even at the height of summer, temperatures don't rise

00:26:42.680 --> 00:26:48.820
above minus 30 degrees. There is no unfrozen water, and to keep themselves clean, they

00:26:48.820 --> 00:27:08.820
have to bathe in snow.

00:27:08.820 --> 00:27:23.440
Snow petrel.

